% Engineering Software with Agentic AI: chapter 1
% Editorial source: composed 19 October 2026.
% All third-party ideas and product facts require a citation in this manuscript.
% Direct third-party quotations: NONE. Author's prior work is cited, not copied verbatim.
% See editorial/CLAIM_LEDGER.csv and editorial/REUSE_REGISTER.md for source-level provenance.
\chapter{An accountable change from day one}\label{ch:first-change}

The operations team has noticed a simple defect in the Treasury routing service introduced in the introduction. A request with the amount \texttt{"-0.01"} receives a standard-queue recommendation. The amount is invalid under the team's rule, so the request should have been rejected before any queue was selected.

You are the developer asked to repair this behavior. Your reviewer has a second concern: valid requests must continue to go to the same queues as before. In particular, EUR\,4,000 must remain below the review threshold, while EUR\,5,000 must still require human review. The job is therefore to add input validation while preserving an existing policy. We will make that distinction explicit before asking an agent to change the code.

This chapter walks through the whole repair. We first read the rule and the function, then predict the faulty result. Next we record the task, run the failure, inspect a proposed implementation and check it against independently maintained examples. At the end, another person should be able to follow what happened from ordinary source files and test reports. The workflow develops the accountable-change method from the author's earlier software-engineering books \parencite{papaliniNDSE2,papaliniNDSE1}.

A later request to lower the threshold to EUR\,3,000 will return in Chapter~11. That is a different engineering problem because it changes the policy and introduces questions about historical records. For now, the threshold stays at EUR\,5,000 and the application remains one small routing function.

\begin{readerbox}{What you will learn}
By the end of the chapter you will be able to explain the routing defect, describe a task that an agent can attempt without guessing the requirement, read the proposed repair line by line and use test results when reviewing it. You will also know why a proposed change, a successful test run and a human acceptance decision are different events.
\end{readerbox}

\section{Understand the request and the function}\label{sec:ch1-system}

The teaching project contains a function named \texttt{assess}. Its input is an amount represented as text, such as \texttt{"4000.00"}. Its output is a \texttt{RoutingDecision}, a small record with three fields: \texttt{route}, \texttt{reason} and \texttt{policy\_version}. A caller could display those fields or use the route to select a work queue. In this chapter we inspect the record directly; we have not built a web interface or payment system.

The \texttt{route} answers ``where should this request go?'' The \texttt{reason} answers ``why?'' The \texttt{policy\_version} identifies the rule used. For a EUR\,4,000 request, the required record is \texttt{STANDARD\_QUEUE}, \texttt{BELOW\_LIMIT}, \texttt{BR-001/v1}. These are fixed labels in the program. They are deliberately more precise than a message such as ``everything looks fine.''

\begin{conceptbox}{Read a business rule as a sequence of questions}
Our fictional rule, \texttt{BR-001/v1}, asks two questions in order. First, can the input be interpreted as a finite, positive amount? If not, return \texttt{REJECTED / INVALID\_AMOUNT}. Second, is that valid amount strictly below EUR\,5,000? If so, use \texttt{STANDARD\_QUEUE / BELOW\_LIMIT}; otherwise use \texttt{HUMAN\_REVIEW / AT\_OR\_ABOVE\_LIMIT}.

``Strictly below'' means that equality belongs to the human-review branch. Every result identifies version 1 of the rule. The service recommends a queue; approval and movement of funds belong to a separate process.
\end{conceptbox}

Write down examples before inspecting the implementation. Doing so separates what the system should do from what the existing code happens to do. The following examples are drawn from the supplied acceptance cases.

\begin{table}[htbp]\centering\small
\caption{Predict the result from the rule before running the code}\label{tab:ch1-input-reasoning}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{.23\textwidth}XX@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Input text} & \textbf{Reasoning} & \textbf{Required route}\\\midrule
\texttt{4000.00} & Positive and below 5,000 & \texttt{STANDARD\_QUEUE}\\
\texttt{5000.00} & Positive and equal to the threshold & \texttt{HUMAN\_REVIEW}\\
\texttt{5001.00} & Positive and above the threshold & \texttt{HUMAN\_REVIEW}\\
\texttt{-0.01}, \texttt{0.00} & Not positive & \texttt{REJECTED}\\
\texttt{not-an-amount} & Cannot be parsed as an amount & \texttt{REJECTED}\\
\texttt{NaN} & A special nonfinite decimal value & \texttt{REJECTED}\\\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

Now read the relevant part of the faulty starter. The omitted surrounding file defines the constants and the result record.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,caption={The starter compares the threshold before validating the amount}]
amount = Decimal(amount_eur)
if amount < THRESHOLD_EUR:
    return RoutingDecision("STANDARD_QUEUE", "BELOW_LIMIT", POLICY_VERSION)
return RoutingDecision("HUMAN_REVIEW", "AT_OR_ABOVE_LIMIT", POLICY_VERSION)
\end{lstlisting}

The first line converts the input text to a decimal value. The \texttt{if} then compares it with the threshold, and \texttt{return} ends the function with the corresponding record. For \texttt{"4000.00"}, those lines happen to implement the intended behavior. For \texttt{"-0.01"}, the conversion also succeeds, and a negative value is less than 5,000. The code consequently selects the standard queue. The missing step is the validity question, not the threshold comparison.

\begin{conceptbox}{Parsing, validation and Decimal}
\textbf{Parsing} converts a representation into a value. \textbf{Validation} asks whether that value is allowed for the task. A negative number can parse successfully and still be an invalid request.

Python's \texttt{Decimal} represents decimal numbers, including values constructed from text. It also represents special values such as \texttt{NaN} (not a number) and infinity. Therefore successful construction alone does not establish a finite amount. The \texttt{is\_finite()} method checks that additional condition \parencite{pythonDecimalCh8}.
\end{conceptbox}

The companion contains both a conforming reference function and a preparation command that creates the deliberately faulty starter. This distinction matters: if you test the packaged reference and see seven passes immediately, you have observed the solution's behavior rather than reproduced the starting defect. Section~\ref{sec:ch1-lab} creates the appropriate study copy.

\begin{practicebox}{Predict two different failures}
Before continuing, predict what the starter does for \texttt{"0.00"} and \texttt{"not-an-amount"}. The first reaches the standard queue because zero is below the threshold. The second fails during decimal construction in the supplied environment. One case produces the wrong answer; the other does not produce the required rejection record at all. A correct repair must address both.
\end{practicebox}

\section{What changes when the assistant can act}\label{sec:ch1-delegation}

An inline completion suggests text while you work. A chat assistant can explain code that you supply. An agentic coding application can also let the model request operations, such as searching for \texttt{assess}, reading its implementation and running a command. The application returns the tool result, and the model can use that result in another step. The term \emph{agentic} describes this arrangement of actions and feedback; it does not guarantee that every chosen action is useful \parencite{papaliniNDACA,papaliniNDLE}.

For the Treasury repair, the agent's task is to propose an implementation of an already stated rule. You remain responsible for supplying the rule, deciding which tools are available and reviewing the proposal. An agent may have the technical ability to edit a threshold constant while lacking authorization to change the threshold. Keeping those two ideas separate makes the task understandable.

\begin{conceptbox}{Capability and authority answer different questions}
\textbf{Capability} means an operation is available: the process can write a Python file. \textbf{Authority} means the task permits that operation for a particular purpose: repair input validation while keeping the threshold unchanged.

Our agent may propose a patch to the routing file. It may not decide that the operations team's rule should instead use EUR\,4,000. A permission setting controls access; the task and its responsible owner establish the intended change.
\end{conceptbox}

\subsection*{Follow one request from model to filesystem}

The \emph{model} produces a response from the context supplied to it. That context can include the task, selected code, instructions, tool descriptions and earlier results. Text is represented as tokens, which are pieces of text rather than a fixed number of characters or whole words. The available context is bounded; an open repository is not automatically present in its entirety. Searching a file and supplying the relevant result are actions performed by the surrounding application \parencite{anthropicContextWindowCR2026}.

We call that surrounding application the \emph{harness}: the IDE, terminal client or service that assembles context, exposes tools, checks applicable permissions, executes permitted requests and returns results. A code block in a model response does not edit a file. A structured tool request is also not an execution result. For a client-side tool, the application must actually run it and return its output; hosted tools may execute in the provider's environment \parencite{anthropicToolCycleCR2026}.

Within that surrounding system, the \emph{orchestrator} coordinates work: it decides which task is ready, dispatches a worker, collects its result and determines whether execution can continue. That responsibility can be implemented by ordinary code, model decisions or a combination. An \emph{agent execution harness} is therefore broader than its scheduler. The companion also uses a \emph{test harness}, meaning the machinery that runs checks. Keep those meanings separate. Finally, \emph{automatic harness engineering} changes the surrounding configuration or code in response to observations. Chapter~7 makes these distinctions executable: Section~\ref{sec:ch7-orchestrator} runs a small controller, and Section~\ref{sec:ch7-automatic-harness} compares fixed and refined harnesses.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Latex,node distance=7mm,every node/.style={font=\scriptsize,align=center},agentstep/.style={draw=accent,rounded corners,fill=pale,text width=.25\linewidth,minimum height=10mm}]
\node[agentstep] (context) {Task + selected sources\\current context};
\node[agentstep,right=of context] (model) {Model\\proposal or tool request};
\node[agentstep,below=9mm of model] (harness) {Harness\\permission + dispatch};
\node[agentstep,left=of harness] (tool) {Tool process\\files, exit code, output};
\draw[->,thick] (context) -- (model);
\draw[->,thick] (model) -- (harness);
\draw[->,thick] (harness) -- (tool);
\draw[->,thick] (tool) -- node[left,font=\scriptsize]{result} (context);
\node[font=\scriptsize,align=center,below=5mm of harness,text width=.43\linewidth] {Stop, deny or escalate here;\\a completion message is not acceptance.};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The first repair's control flow. The model proposes; the configured runtime executes. This original schematic is not a captured vendor-client trace.}
\label{fig:ch1-model-harness}
\end{figure}

\begin{examplebox}{Who does what during the first test?}
The model may request the baseline test command. The harness checks whether that command is permitted and starts Python if it is. Python calls the faulty function with the selected inputs and returns failures. The harness supplies that output to the model. The model can now propose an input guard, but the proposed text must still be applied and tested.

If the command was refused, there is no test result to interpret. If the command ran and a test failed, there is a useful observation about the candidate. This is why the transcript distinguishes a requested action from an executed action and its result.
\end{examplebox}

\Needspace{12\baselineskip}
Here is an annotated request for our first repair. The labels are explanatory, not a product's wire format:

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={One test request, three separate observations}]
PROPOSED ACTION: run the reader's baseline command
  python3 reader_side/tools/run_acceptance.py \
    --output reader_side/evidence/CH01_BASELINE_RESULT.json
EXECUTION: the permitted Python process runs the selected cases
OBSERVATION: exit status and saved report identify pass/fail/error
NEXT DECISION: inspect the invalid-input failures; propose a patch
STOP CONDITION: unexpected scope, ambiguous behavior, or review ready
\end{lstlisting}

The harness may include the returned failure in a subsequent model call. The next response may propose a patch, request another observation or ask for clarification. Persisted source files, reports and conversation state belong to the surrounding tools and service; do not assume that a fresh session contains yesterday's context or that a remembered summary is a current source. Chapter~2 makes these locations explicit. Chapter~6 teaches what to retain, retrieve, revise or discard \parencite{anthropicContext2025}.

For this exercise, stop after the bounded repair and review package. If the model says \enquote{done} before the gate ran, there is a proposal without the required observation. If the tool failed, its error is feedback to diagnose, not an instruction to loosen permissions or replace the expected result.

\section{Record intent before handing off implementation}\label{sec:ch1-intent}

Before delegating, write a few sentences that answer four questions: what is wrong, what should change, what must stay the same and how the result will be checked. This is the \emph{intent record}. For our example, the intent is to reject invalid amounts without changing the routing of valid amounts.

We assign the record the identifier \texttt{INT-001}, meaning the first intent in this teaching project. The \emph{decision owner} is the person entitled to settle uncertainty about the required behavior. Here that role is represented by a fictional Operations policy owner. The agent receives the approved rule as input; it is not asked to invent financial policy.

A \emph{non-goal} names a plausible addition that this task does not require. For example, adding a web API may be useful later, but it would make this repair harder to review. An \emph{acceptance condition} describes an outcome the reviewer expects to see. Rejecting \texttt{"-0.01"} while preserving the EUR\,5,000 boundary is such a condition. The following compact record makes these decisions visible.

\begin{table}[htbp]\centering\small
\caption{INT-001: the initial delegated change contract}\label{tab:ch1-contract}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.24\textwidth}X@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Field} & \textbf{Recorded decision}\\\midrule
Decision owner & Synthetic Operations policy owner; real implementations require a named authority\\
Scope & Reject invalid input amounts with a stable reason code\\
Keep unchanged & Threshold remains EUR 5,000; valid positive routing behavior remains unchanged\\
Non-goals & No ledger, persistence, UI, API, fund movement or historical replay in Chapter~1\\
Delegated authority & Propose source edits; do not revise policy or acceptance cases\\
Evidence & External acceptance cases and human review of the diff\\
Escalation & Stop if the meaning of ``invalid'' or a new edge case is uncertain\\\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The delegation boundary is not an exercise in paperwork. The record prevents a tempting expansion of scope: an agent could reasonably decide that the threshold should be configurable, add a new data-access layer or redesign the API. Those might be useful later, but they are not justified by \texttt{INT-001}. Refusing unnecessary complexity is part of finishing the requested work.

For this repair, keep the smallest adequate subset of the earlier workflow: who decides, what changes, what stays fixed and what counts as evidence \parencite{papaliniNDSE1,papaliniNDSDD1,papaliniNDSDD2}. Use the \path{practice/agent_workflow/proportionate_controls.md} card to keep these answers in one short record. Do not create a separate planning platform for a one-function change.

\begin{readerbox}{Add a control when the consequence changes}
\textbf{This repair:} one intent, one file diff, seven selected cases and a review verdict. \textbf{A persistent policy change (Chapter~11):} also identify the old policy and retained history; test replay, retry and migration because new code can affect existing records. \textbf{A release or operational action (Chapters~10--12):} also bind the exact artifact and target state, identify the authorized operator, and rehearse recovery because an action can outlive the editing session. Sensitive data additionally needs an explicit read/export boundary. The amount of evidence grows with these consequences; tests never supply the missing authority.
\end{readerbox}

\section{Run the baseline, then delegate a bounded repair}
\label{sec:ch1-lab}
\input{entrycards/chapter01.tex}
\par\medskip

\begin{readerbox}{Suggested lab time: 30--45 minutes, plus tool setup}
\textbf{You need:} Python 3.10 or later and the Chapter~1 companion; the reader guide distinguishes the declared minimum from the version used for this review. An authenticated coding-agent account is optional for the independent tests; use one for the delegated-implementation part. \textbf{You will produce:} a candidate source diff, seven acceptance-test results, an execution record, and a review verdict. \textbf{Safety boundary:} no real Treasury systems, credentials or payment data.
\end{readerbox}

\begin{conceptbox}{What the checker compares}
An \textbf{acceptance test} supplies an input and compares the observed result with the behavior required by the task. The source of that expected answer is sometimes called a \textbf{test oracle}. In this lab the oracle comes from the reader-controlled policy and cases, rather than being inferred from the faulty function.

A \textbf{baseline} is the starting state and its observed behavior. We keep its report so that the repair can be compared with the original failure. A \textbf{hash} or \textbf{digest} is a fingerprint computed from file bytes. Comparing fingerprints helps detect whether the rule or tests changed between runs; it does not establish that the rule itself is correct.
\end{conceptbox}

\subsection*{Step 1: Inspect the baseline and locate the authority}

From the book package root, prepare a new working copy, then enter it:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
python3 companion/reader_side/tools/prepare_chapter01.py \
  --destination chapter01_work
cd chapter01_work
\end{lstlisting}

The first command creates a separate full working copy and seeds its routing function with the known defect. The \texttt{--destination} option names that copy; \texttt{cd} makes it the current directory for subsequent commands. The destination must not already exist or be inside \path{companion/}, so earlier work is not overwritten.

The general reader helper offers an alternative spelling from the package root: \texttt{python3 companion/reader\_start.py new 1 --destination chapter01\_work}. It calls the same preparation routine. Choose that route or the command above once; do not run both for the same destination.

Read \path{reader_side/intent/INT-001.md}, \path{repo/src/treasury/routing.py}, and \path{reader_side/acceptance/test_routing.py}. The intent is an educational rule; acceptance cases cover positive, threshold and invalid amounts.

Ask yourself what the original implementation would do with the string \texttt{"-0.01"}. The code is concise and looks correct for ordinary positive inputs. That is precisely why we need an explicit edge-case contract. A successful build would not expose the missing business decision.

\subsection*{Step 2: Establish an observable red baseline}

From this working copy's root, run:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
python3 reader_side/tools/run_acceptance.py \
  --output reader_side/evidence/CH01_BASELINE_RESULT.json
\end{lstlisting}

The \texttt{--output} option selects the report filename. The report is a JSON file, a structured text record that you can inspect in an editor. The supplied starter has seven tests. When executed in the verified preparation environment for this chapter, three passed, two failed because negative and zero amounts were routed, and two raised exceptions for malformed and nonfinite inputs. The command therefore returned a nonzero exit status. This is \emph{expected red}, not a corrupted installation. The command writes the named baseline report with the test result, Python version and SHA-256 hashes of the reader-controlled intent and acceptance files. Later runs without \texttt{--output} write \path{reader_side/evidence/CH01_ROUTING_RESULT.json}, preserving this red baseline.

Read the errors. A failed assertion means the function returned the wrong route. An exception means the function could not represent a required invalid-input decision. Both demonstrate a gap, but they imply slightly different implementation work. Keep that distinction in your review notes.

\subsection*{Step 3: Hand an agent one bounded task}

Choose a coding client you already have, or first study the protected reference below if no client is available. Section~\ref{sec:ch1-tools} gives the optional product routes. A coding-agent surface is simply the interface through which you work, such as an editor panel or a terminal session. GitHub Copilot's IDE agent mode is one documented route \parencite{githubCopilotAgent2026}; a Claude Code, Codex or Cursor session can be used with the same text after reviewing its documented permission settings \parencite{anthropicClaudeStart2026,openaiCodexWorkflow2026,cursorRules2026}.

Open \path{repo/} as the \emph{writable} task workspace if your harness allows you to restrict it that way. Keep \path{reader_side/acceptance/} and \path{reader_side/intent/} separately controlled. The text in \texttt{AGENTS.md} is guidance, not a filesystem permission boundary; a tool that can write the parent directory may still alter protected material. Do not grant a broader tool scope than you intend.

Give the agent the following task request, supplying the relevant intent and test descriptions as inputs. This is an original teaching prompt, not a universal optimal prompt:

\begin{readerbox}{Task supplied to the coding agent}
Implement \texttt{INT-001} in \path{src/treasury/routing.py}. Reject zero, negative, malformed and nonfinite EUR values with \texttt{REJECTED / INVALID\_AMOUNT}. Preserve the existing routing results for valid positive values and the \texttt{BR-001/v1} policy identifier. Modify no other file. Do not change the threshold, acceptance cases, intent or tool permissions. If a behavior is ambiguous, stop and ask. Return the proposed diff and the checks you ran; do not claim the change is accepted.
\end{readerbox}

Review the proposed actions as they appear. The point of the task is not to set an autonomy record. It is to make the smallest correct increment visible and recoverable. If the agent wants to install a dependency, redesign the router or modify the independent tests, ask why; none is necessary for this example.

\subsection*{Step 4: Verify independently and inspect the candidate}

After the candidate code is available, return to the working copy's root and run \texttt{python3} with \path{reader_side/tools/run_acceptance.py}. Do not let the generated implementation rewrite the expected answers. Compare the intent and acceptance hashes with the baseline. The protected reference, \path{reader_side/reference_solution/routing.py}, passes all seven tests; its complete source makes the example reproducible without an agent account.

A green run is necessary for this exercise but not a proof of every property of the router. The suite does not test persistence, concurrency, international payment formatting, network security or release to production. These functions do not exist in the chapter's code. Record them as outside scope or future questions, not as invisible successes.

\subsection*{Read the repair in three stages}

The protected reference supplies one possible implementation. Study it after making your own prediction or inspecting your agent's proposal. Its validation prefix is shown below; the original threshold branches follow it unchanged.

\Needspace{9\baselineskip}
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,caption={The reference validates construction, finiteness and positivity before routing}]
try:
    amount = Decimal(amount_eur)
except (InvalidOperation, ValueError, TypeError):
    return RoutingDecision("REJECTED", "INVALID_AMOUNT", POLICY_VERSION)
if not amount.is_finite() or amount <= 0:
    return RoutingDecision("REJECTED", "INVALID_AMOUNT", POLICY_VERSION)
\end{lstlisting}

First, the \texttt{try} block attempts to parse the input. The listed exception handlers turn the corresponding construction failures into the agreed rejection record. Second, \texttt{is\_finite()} rules out special values such as \texttt{NaN}. Python evaluates the \texttt{or} expression from left to right: once the nonfinite condition is true, it does not need to perform the comparison on the right. Third, a finite amount at or below zero is rejected. Only a finite, positive amount reaches the inherited threshold comparison.

Trace \texttt{"-0.01"} through this version. Parsing succeeds; the value is finite; the nonpositive check is true; the function returns \texttt{REJECTED}. Now trace \texttt{"5000.00"}. Parsing and validation succeed, the existing strict-less-than comparison is false, and the function returns \texttt{HUMAN\_REVIEW}. The repair changes the invalid-input path while preserving the required boundary result.

You may produce a different implementation with the same behavior. Compare it with the rule and cases, rather than judging correctness by textual similarity to the reference. In later cumulative exercises we adopt exact reference bytes to keep the teaching state reproducible; that is a laboratory convention, not a rule that all correct implementations must look alike.

Inspect the code for the following engineering properties:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item malformed input is handled without turning unexpected parsing failures into a normal route;
\item negative, zero and nonfinite values are rejected explicitly;
\item the EUR 5,000 policy constant remains unchanged;
\item values below and at the threshold retain their distinct behavior;
\item the policy identifier is still reported for every decision;
\item only the intended source file changed; the independently controlled acceptance material did not.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{warningbox}{Passing tests do not make a test independent}
A test written or rewritten by the same agent that implemented the behavior may encode the same incorrect assumption. In this lab the acceptance cases and intent record are separated from the agent's stated writable scope, and the reader controls the verification run. This is a useful classroom separation, \emph{not an operating-system sandbox}. Production enforcement requires actual permissions, isolated workspaces or protected CI identities, according to the risk involved. Chapter~9 develops the verifier question in detail, building on \textcite{papaliniNDSE2} and the loop-control treatment in \textcite{papaliniNDLE}.
\end{warningbox}

\subsection*{Step 5: Record a human verdict}

Copy \path{reader_side/templates/CH01_REVIEW_TEMPLATE.md} to \path{reader_side/evidence/CH01_REVIEW.md}. Fill in the candidate identity, environment and agent/model versions if an agent was used, before-and-after hashes, failing baseline, green result, protected-file inspection and any unresolved question. Decide \emph{ready for review}, \emph{accept}, or \emph{reject}. Do not use these statuses interchangeably: technical tests passing may make a change ready for human review without assigning the business owner decision to the agent.

If you are doing the lab alone, mark the policy authority as \emph{synthetic}. Do not imply that a genuine Treasury stakeholder approved a teaching exercise. The purpose is to rehearse what a real delegation agreement would have to identify.

\begin{examplebox}{A short, useful review verdict}
A review note for this teaching repair could say: ``The candidate rejects the four invalid-input examples in the supplied cases. The three valid boundary examples retain their required routes and policy version. The intent and test files are unchanged. I have inspected the one-file diff. This proposal is ready for the designated reviewer; persistence and payment execution are outside this exercise.''

Each sentence has a job: behavior changed, behavior preserved, expectations preserved, source inspected and next decision identified. Write those sentences from your own run. Do not copy a passing result from the reference into a report about an untested candidate.
\end{examplebox}

\section{Understand what the evidence proves}\label{sec:ch1-evidence}

Imagine two runs. The first returns a patch with a green unit test, but also changes the threshold from EUR 5,000 to EUR 4,000. The second preserves the threshold and meets every independent acceptance case. A simple metric called ``tests passed'' cannot distinguish the two if the first agent rewrote its tests. The engineering decision requires a link from intent to behavior and then to independently controlled evidence.

A minimal evidence record should answer: What change was requested? Which baseline was examined? Which source candidate was produced? Which verifier ran against which candidate? Which checks passed, failed or were not run? Who decided the result could be accepted? Which uncertainties remain?

This can be done with ordinary files and Git. An elaborate evidence platform is unnecessary for a one-function repair. However, the fields need to exist even when the chosen agent offers a polished summary. Such a summary is a useful index to the work, not a substitute for the underlying artifacts.

\begin{table}[htbp]\centering\small
\caption{Observed ChatGPT workspace repair, 20 October 2026}\label{tab:ch1-evidence}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.27\textwidth}X@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Question} & \textbf{Recorded answer}\\\midrule
Intention & \texttt{INT-001}; invalid input handling; do not change the threshold\\
Baseline & Supplied starter, 7 tests: 3 pass, 2 failure, 2 error\\
Candidate & Assistant-authored parsing and finite/positive guards; exact patch retained\\
Verifier & Same seven owner-side cases, Python 3.12.14; intent and acceptance hashes unchanged\\
Observed result & 7 pass; no failures or errors after the repair\\
Limits & Reference seen beforehand; no independent human review or named vendor-client comparison\\
Release state & Ready for human review; no deployment or payment authority\\\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The original checkpoint's hashes and test transcripts relate only to its recorded files and environment. Hash equality can show whether two files are byte-identical; it does not show whether their expectations are correct. The reviewer must still inspect the authority behind the expectations. This is why evidence quality is an engineering concern, not simply a logging concern.

Inspect \path{practice/agent_workflow/runs/CP01_CHATGPT_20261020/}. Its task, raw red and green reports, command records, baseline source, candidate and patch let you follow the observed contribution: catch invalid decimal construction; reject nonfinite and nonpositive values; retain the existing threshold branches. The assistant had already read the teaching solution during editorial work. This therefore demonstrates a recorded repair, not an unseen capability test. Recorded command intervals are not human effort or a speed benchmark; billing and token counts are unknown. The exact patch names one source file, but no complete pre-edit tree snapshot was captured, so it also makes no claim of independently enforced edit scope. Use it as a worked example, then retain equivalent evidence for your own attempt.

\section{Choose a client for your own attempt}\label{sec:ch1-tools}

Choose a tool that can perform this task with an inspectable diff and appropriate permissions; there is no need to adopt a permanent stack first. The earlier tool-selection material supplies historical context, not a current feature or pricing comparison \parencite{papaliniNDSE1,atlasV01B021}.

For the next documented classroom trials, this edition selects VS Code with GitHub Copilot as the primary editor path and Claude Code as the contrasting terminal path. This is a tutorial choice, not a measured ranking. Their installed versions and same-task runs remain pending in the runtime matrix. The portable offline exercise can be completed without either account; a reader using another client should record its own setup and evidence. No relative-performance claim follows from the selection.

