\section{Software engineering has a wider boundary than code generation}\label{sec:ch1-wider-boundary}

A software change begins when someone notices a difference between a system's behavior and what its stakeholders need. That difference may appear as a bug report, a customer complaint, an operational event, a new regulation, or a product decision. Code is one possible answer, but the first job is to understand the question.

A professional engineer may have to establish the current behavior, identify whose interpretation is binding, compare designs, isolate a failure, produce a safe change, review evidence, coordinate a release, and later explain the consequences of the decision. Artificial intelligence can assist with almost all these activities. Its assistance does not make them the same activity.

The empirical literature gives a useful warning against measuring only typing speed. In three field experiments spanning 4,867 developers, randomized access to a code-completion assistant was associated with an estimated 26.08\% increase in the study's completed-task outcome, with a reported standard error of 10.3 percentage points. The result concerns that outcome and the tools and organizations examined; it is not automatically a 26\% reduction in end-to-end delivery time \parencite{cui2026}. In a different randomized study, 16 experienced open-source developers completed 246 tasks on projects they knew well. In that particular 2025 setting, allowing AI tools increased measured completion time by an estimated 19\%. The authors investigated potential explanations and cautioned against simple generalization beyond the setting \parencite{becker2025}.

These results are not contradictory instructions to adopt or reject AI. They concern different people, tasks, tools, environments, and outcomes. An engineering method must keep the denominators and the acceptance boundary visible. A faster code-editing step can coexist with slower review. More completed tasks need not mean more reliable deployment. A pleasant tool experience is not itself evidence of an accepted outcome. We return to this issue in Chapter~14.

\begin{readerbox}{An engineering unit of work}
For this book, a \textbf{change} is not complete because the agent has stopped or the diff looks plausible. It becomes a candidate for acceptance when the authorized intention is clear, the implementation is reviewable, relevant evidence has been produced, known risks have been recorded, and the designated owner can decide what happens next. This is a proposed working definition, not a universally validated productivity metric.
\end{readerbox}

The vocabulary matters. A tool can generate a \emph{proposal}. A local test can establish a \emph{particular observed property}. A reviewer can decide the proposal is \emph{ready for a wider review}. Product or operational authority can \emph{accept} it. These are different states. If an organization collapses them into a single ``done'' flag, faster generation can propagate unreviewed errors before the missing decision is noticed.

\section{Measure the whole work, not the agent's typing speed}\label{sec:ch1-measure}

This is a good moment to establish a baseline for the book's later economics. If you use a live agent, record a small set of raw observations: your time reading and clarifying the request; agent runtime; your time examining the diff; your time diagnosing failures; any repeated attempts; and whether the final candidate was accepted. For a manual run, record the same outcome without inventing an agent runtime.

These are observations about \emph{one task in one environment}. They cannot by themselves establish that Copilot is faster than Cursor, that agents improve quality, or that a company would earn a particular return. They are nevertheless useful because they prevent token counts, apparent fluency and volume of generated code from being mistaken for accepted engineering outcomes. The economics of controlled loops are explored in \textcite{papaliniNDLE}; the broader evidence and adoption limitations are treated in \textcite{papaliniNDSE2}. We will extend the instrumentation in Chapter~14.

\begin{readerbox}{A simple comparison protocol}
If you want to try a second coding agent, begin from a fresh copy of the same starter. Give it the same \texttt{INT-001} instruction, the same acceptance requirements and comparable tool permissions. Record agent version, model, time, prompts, attempts and acceptance result. Do not call a single paired comparison a benchmark. Record divergences in process, not just the finish time. Never allow the second run to begin from the first run's repaired source.
\end{readerbox}

\section{When the agent offers to do too much}\label{sec:ch1-overreach}

Successful agentic engineering includes the ability to decline a plausible expansion of the task. Suppose the agent observes that \texttt{Decimal} input might arrive from a REST API in the future. It proposes adding FastAPI, a database schema, authentication middleware and new configuration files. The proposal may be technically sensible in a different project. Here, it violates the explicit non-goals and increases the verification surface.

A second agent may propose replacing the acceptance file with tests that check only that \texttt{assess} returns some object. This would reduce failures but would abandon the required behavior. A third may say the input \texttt{NaN} is impossible because an upstream UI never sends it, even though the teaching interface accepts strings and no upstream validation has been demonstrated. That is an unverified assumption.

Each example calls for a different response: decline unrequested architecture, preserve the external oracle, or request evidence about the supposed upstream contract. The aim is not distrust for its own sake. It is to allocate judgment to the place where a hidden assumption becomes consequential.

The strongest version of this discipline is not a particularly stern prompt. It is the combination of appropriately scoped tools, protected expectations, reliable feedback, records of intended behavior, and a human prepared to stop a run. These mechanisms will become more sophisticated in Chapters~2, 7 and~9. They should be introduced only when a real failure mode makes their value clear.

\section{Exercises and transfer questions}\label{sec:ch1-exercises}

\textbf{Exercise A: Explain the reference candidate.} Run the acceptance script with \texttt{--candidate} \path{reader_side/reference_solution/routing.py} and a distinct \texttt{--output} file to preserve your own candidate report. Without asking an agent, write a five-sentence account of why a negative number was previously misrouted, where the fix is implemented, why \texttt{Decimal("NaN")} needs special handling, which positive boundary is unchanged, and what the seven passing tests do not establish. Compare your account with the protected reference source and the reader-side acceptance cases.

\textbf{Exercise B: A deliberately ambiguous request.} Someone proposes: \enquote{Please approve requests below EUR 3,000 automatically.} Do not edit the code. Identify the ambiguity between \emph{routing} and \emph{authorizing}. Write down who must decide what the request means, which part conflicts with the approved \texttt{INT-001} task, and what boundary your current repair must preserve while the new request is unresolved. Your output is a short clarification record. Chapter~11 develops this case further after introducing stored decisions and policy history.

\textbf{Exercise C: The attractive false green.} Imagine the agent changes both the router and the acceptance tests so that \texttt{"-0.01"} is labeled \texttt{STANDARD\_QUEUE}. Explain why the test suite might be green while the change still violates \texttt{BR-001/v1}. List one technical permission mechanism and one organizational decision needed to prevent silent acceptance. Distinguish an instruction written in Markdown from an enforced write restriction.

\textbf{Exercise D: Compare tools cautiously (optional).} Run the same \texttt{INT-001} from an untouched starter with two available coding agents. Compare the exact changes, extra files, attempts, failed tests, execution settings, total human review work and final verdict. State your uncertainty. Do not conclude that one tool is globally better from this one exercise.

\begin{practicebox}{Check your explanations}
For Exercise A, the essential reasoning is that the old code confused ``less than the threshold'' with ``valid.'' The fix validates before comparing, and the exact-threshold example still requires review. A passing result covers the selected cases; it does not demonstrate behavior of a database or deployment that the chapter never built.

For Exercise B, first ask whether \enquote{approve} means choosing a queue or granting permission to execute a payment. The latter would introduce a new capability and owner decision. The policy owner must also authorize any threshold change: \texttt{INT-001} only permits repairing invalid-input handling. Until that separate decision is made, preserve the EUR\,5,000 boundary and its tests. For Exercise C, a green test is unhelpful when its expected answer was changed to agree with the bug. Protected write permissions and a reviewer who checks the approved rule address different parts of that failure.
\end{practicebox}

\section{What to carry into the next chapter}\label{sec:ch1-next}

You have completed an entire small engineering trajectory: a synthetic requirement with a decision owner, an observed defect, a bounded delegation, a repaired candidate, a separately controlled test run, and a review verdict. None requires a complex multi-agent architecture. What matters is that source edits, claims about the edits and evidence for acceptance remain distinguishable.

Chapter~2 turns this ad hoc working arrangement into a portable engineering workspace: repository instructions, agent skills, tool interfaces, permissions and repeatable checks. It will use concrete 2026 products, but each product configuration will be connected to an engineering property. When product interfaces change, the underlying engineering property tells us what must be preserved.

