\chapter{Plan work, delegate tasks and coordinate responsibility}
\label{ch:plan-delegation}

An operator can now read a proposed explanation of a Treasury routing decision. For a valid amount below EUR~5,000, the service recommends the standard queue; at or above that amount, it recommends human review. The team has also explored two ways of producing that explanation. Before asking a coding agent to finish the feature, someone must decide which design to adopt.

That is where we resume the case study. The earlier chapters gave us a behavior specification, two prototypes and a packet of relevant sources. The direct adapter is the proposed design, but its architecture decision record, or \emph{ADR}, still awaits the technical owner's decision. The record is called \texttt{ADR-005}; the name helps us find it, while its status tells us what work may follow. The prototype's successful tests are useful input to that decision \parencite{papaliniNDSE2,papaliniNDLE,atlasV04B090}.

Suppose the next request is \enquote{Implement the chosen adapter and get the feature released.} Before dividing the work, read the word \emph{chosen} carefully. The source record says \emph{proposed}. We can investigate the design now, but the implementation task needs a decision that has not yet been made. Planning means making that dependency visible so that a fast worker does not run ahead of the team.

This chapter teaches how to delegate in that situation. We will first describe tasks in ordinary language, decide which can proceed together, and identify the person who may unblock the next step. We will then express the same plan as data. Only after those ideas are clear will the optional runtime exercises turn them into running processes. Earlier task-decomposition and coordination treatments provide the conceptual background \parencite{atlasV04B089,atlasV04B095}.

\begin{conceptbox}{A task graph connects work to its prerequisites}
A \emph{task} is a piece of work with a purpose and an observable result. A \emph{dependency} says that another result or decision is required first. A \emph{task graph} records these relationships. Two investigations can start together when neither needs the other's answer. An implementation must wait when it needs a design decision. The graph explains the order; assigning a worker does not remove a dependency.
\end{conceptbox}

\begin{readerbox}{What you will learn}
You will write a bounded task, distinguish a dependency from an approval, choose when parallel work is useful, and prepare a handoff that another person can use. The first exercise preserves an accurate pending plan. The continuing example then supplies explicit fictional owner decisions and validates the transition to implementation. You can also run a small orchestrator and examine an automatic harness-improvement experiment.
\end{readerbox}

