Three simple mistakes help us inspect a graph. A \emph{dangling dependency} points to a nonexistent task, so the prerequisite can never arrive. A \emph{cycle} occurs when A waits for B while B waits, directly or indirectly, for A. A \emph{false-ready task} appears executable only because the plan omitted a necessary prerequisite, such as the owner decision. The planning gate detects selected examples of these mistakes. Here, a \emph{gate} is a program that checks stated conditions and returns findings; it does not decide the business policy.

A running coordinator also needs rules for cancellation, retries, interrupted processes and changes to shared state. We will introduce those concerns in the optional controller exercise. The broader runtime treatment is developed in \emph{Non-Deterministic Loop Engineering} \parencite{papaliniNDLE}.

\section{Choose the smallest useful delegation topology}
\label{sec:ch7-topology}

A \emph{delegation topology} is the arrangement of workers and information flow: one worker, several investigators reporting to a coordinator, or a sequence in which one output becomes the next input. Start by identifying the missing information. The number of available agents is a later consideration.

Our case has two useful questions. The specification investigator asks whether the proposed explanation preserves the agreed behavior. The architecture investigator asks which trade-offs and unanswered questions the two prototypes leave. Each can inspect the existing sources without waiting for the other. Their findings can therefore be gathered in parallel, then read together by the owner. This pattern is often called \emph{fan out and join}: distribute independent questions, then combine their results.

Coding is different at this point. Both possible writers would need the same unresolved architecture decision and might edit the same implementation. Starting them together would add conflicting changes without supplying the missing decision. The reference plan consequently chooses \texttt{BOUNDED\_SINGLE\_WRITER}: two read-only investigations are possible now, and one narrowly scoped writer may be authorized later.

The original planning demonstration groups ready tasks into logical rounds. With one slot, it produces \texttt{[T07-01], [T07-02]}; with two slots, \texttt{[T07-01,T07-02]}. This tells us which work could overlap. It does not run the workers or tell us how much time a real team would save. Use the table and \path{practice/agent_workflow/pattern_selector.md} to choose a pattern from the task's dependencies.

