\section{Give every delegated worker a readable, enforceable boundary}
\label{sec:ch7-boundaries}

A task boundary has an explanatory part and an enforcement part. The explanatory part tells the worker what to do. The enforcement part limits what its tools and execution account can actually do. If we describe an investigator as read-only but give it a shell that can rewrite the repository, our description has not removed that ability \parencite{papaliniNDLE,papaliniNDSDD2}.

For the two investigators, the proposed write set is empty. For the later implementation task, it contains one path: \path{src/treasury/architecture/direct.py}. The router, policy, expected test results, specification and architecture record belong outside that task's edits. This lets a reviewer ask a practical question: did the worker change only the file it was asked to change?

\begin{conceptbox}{Instructions, checks and restrictions have different jobs}
An instruction says \enquote{do not edit the expected results}. A comparison after the work can detect that the expected-results file changed. An access restriction can prevent that worker from writing the file in the first place. Use the words \emph{requested}, \emph{checked} and \emph{prevented} for these different observations.

The companion's owner-run scope guard and hashes demonstrate selected comparisons. They are not an operating-system sandbox. A real agent environment needs its own permission configuration when prevention is required.
\end{conceptbox}

Write the assignment so that another person can inspect it: \enquote{Read INT-001 and SPEC-004/v1, report mismatches, and leave policy and tests unchanged.} The identifiers locate the two source records already introduced. The expected output is a report of observations. Similarly, an independent reviewer needs expectations controlled separately from the proposed patch; a second agent reading only the maker's self-tests can inherit the same error \parencite{papaliniNDSE2}.

